      <h2>1. The Two Pillars of Indian Educational Compensation</h2>
      <p>
        Teaching compensation in the Indian public sector is organized across two distinct administrative domains, each responding to statutory frameworks established by the Central Government:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>School Education (KVS, NVS, CTSA, and State Schools):</strong> Governed by the Ministry of Education and conforming directly to standard Central Pay Commission civil levels: Primary Teachers (<strong>PRT in Level 6</strong>), Trained Graduate Teachers (<strong>TGT in Level 7</strong>), and Post Graduate Teachers (<strong>PGT in Level 8</strong>).</li>
        <li><strong>Higher & University Education (UGC, AICTE, IITs, NITs, and Central Universities):</strong> Governed by the <strong>University Grants Commission (UGC)</strong> under Ministry of Education directives. Higher education faculty are structured into distinct <strong>Academic Levels (AL-10 to AL-15)</strong> that incorporate entry pay enhancements and research-linked incentives beyond standard civil scales.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        With the anticipated rollout of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission</strong>, teaching federations (such as the All India Federation of University and College Teachers' Organisations - AIFUCTO, and school teacher associations) are focusing on critical policy imperatives: closing the gap between school educators and gazetted officers, expanding Career Advancement Scheme (CAS) promotion pipelines, and restoring advance increment benefits for terminal research degrees.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Complete School & University Faculty Pay Matrix (Levels 6 to 17)</h2>
