      <h2>6. School Education Cadres: PRT, TGT, and PGT Structures</h2>
      <p>
        School teachers in central autonomous bodies (Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan - KVS, Navodaya Vidyalaya Samiti - NVS) and union territory administrations are structured across three primary teaching bands:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Primary Teachers (PRT - Level 6, Starting Basic ₹35,400):</strong> Instruct foundational and primary classes (Grades 1 to 5). Minimum qualifications require a D.El.Ed/B.Ed and passing the Central Teacher Eligibility Test (CTET Paper 1). Under the 8th CPC, starting pay is projected to expand to between <strong>₹79,650 and ₹1,01,244</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Trained Graduate Teachers (TGT - Level 7, Starting Basic ₹44,900):</strong> Handle secondary classes (Grades 6 to 10). Minimum qualifications mandate a Bachelor’s degree in the teaching subject, B.Ed, and CTET Paper 2. Projected 8th CPC entry basic pay ranges from <strong>₹1,01,025 to ₹1,28,414</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Post Graduate Teachers (PGT - Level 8, Starting Basic ₹47,600):</strong> Responsible for senior secondary instruction (Grades 11 and 12). Candidates must hold a Master’s degree in the subject with a B.Ed. Projected 8th CPC entry basic pay ranges from <strong>₹1,07,100 to ₹1,36,136</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>7. Core Demands Ahead of the 8th Pay Commission for Educators</h2>
      <p>
        Ahead of 8th CPC deliberations, national teaching federations and university unions have articulated several shared priorities:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Upgrading School Educator Entry Scales:</strong> Demands from school teacher unions emphasize that primary and secondary teaching requires rigorous pre-service diplomas and national testing. They advocate upgrading PRTs from Level 6 to Level 7, and TGTs from Level 7 to Level 8, to reflect their educational responsibilities.</li>
        <li><strong>Restoring Slashed Ph.D. In-Service Increments:</strong> While pre-service entry increments were retained, regulations following the 7th CPC restricted certain in-service advance increments. University faculty federations demand the restoration of automatic, compounded advance increments for every higher research credential completed during tenure.</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement Age Harmonization:</strong> Central University faculty currently retire at <strong>65 years of age</strong> (with provisions for re-employment up to 70 in specialized research positions). School teachers, however, remain tied to the civil superannuation age of <strong>60 years</strong>. Federations are urging the government to consider raising the superannuation age for school educators to 62 or 65 years to retain experienced mentors.</li>
      </ul>

